1
00:00:00,151 --> 00:00:04,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's just... first, maybe, just... sati.

2
00:00:05,014 --> 00:00:05,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati.

3
00:00:05,514 --> 00:00:06,835
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

4
00:00:07,755 --> 00:00:09,056
[SPEAKER_00]: What is sati?

5
00:00:09,236 --> 00:00:09,656
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

6
00:00:10,357 --> 00:00:10,497
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

7
00:00:10,657 --> 00:00:11,057
[SPEAKER_02]: What is sati?

8
00:00:11,077 --> 00:00:13,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So-called mindfulness.

9
00:00:13,799 --> 00:00:18,501
[SPEAKER_02]: What is sati and why it's better to not translate it as mindfulness?

10
00:00:18,561 --> 00:00:18,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

11
00:00:19,322 --> 00:00:19,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

12
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:29,905
[SPEAKER_00]: As you're saying, it's better to translate it, well, this is the option, as recollection.

13
00:00:31,386 --> 00:00:31,746
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

14
00:00:31,826 --> 00:00:33,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollectedness, I suppose.

15
00:00:33,727 --> 00:00:34,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollectedness.

16
00:00:34,727 --> 00:00:36,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's about remembering something.

17
00:00:37,089 --> 00:00:37,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

18
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:39,110
[SPEAKER_00]: A specific thing.

19
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:45,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Remembering is not doing.

20
00:00:47,675 --> 00:00:50,337
[SPEAKER_02]: So what is the, what is the nature then?

21
00:00:51,371 --> 00:00:55,753
[SPEAKER_02]: So, given that sati, even in the suttas, it's used as memory.

22
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:57,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

23
00:00:58,075 --> 00:00:58,995
[SPEAKER_02]: One who remembers things.

24
00:00:59,095 --> 00:01:00,556
[SPEAKER_02]: One who remembers things, yeah.

25
00:01:01,497 --> 00:01:06,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the nature of recollecting?

26
00:01:09,781 --> 00:01:12,823
[SPEAKER_02]: What is a necessary condition for you to recollect something?

27
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:18,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

28
00:01:20,735 --> 00:01:25,617
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in order for you to recollect something... A knowledge.

29
00:01:26,458 --> 00:01:28,078
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to know what you're recollecting.

30
00:01:28,419 --> 00:01:36,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you recollect something you never ever experienced or or came across or thought of?

31
00:01:36,942 --> 00:01:38,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you recollect such things?

32
00:01:39,203 --> 00:01:39,363
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

33
00:01:40,084 --> 00:01:40,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

34
00:01:40,624 --> 00:01:41,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's...

35
00:01:43,685 --> 00:01:45,286
[SPEAKER_02]: The necessary condition for sati

36
00:01:46,112 --> 00:01:51,955
[SPEAKER_02]: is at least a degree of some discernment of something, because there is nothing to be recollected.

37
00:01:53,936 --> 00:01:55,277
[SPEAKER_01]: How can recollection take place?

38
00:01:55,858 --> 00:01:56,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

39
00:01:57,538 --> 00:02:12,307
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the effort of sati is the effort of recollecting either what you discern or recollecting that which you basically learn that discern... recollecting what you learn that discernment is.

40
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:23,847
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, even if somebody who doesn't necessarily have the values of a sotāpanna or somebody in training, to recollect them...

41
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:26,529
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of sati is the nature of recollecting.

42
00:02:26,589 --> 00:02:28,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recollect the purpose of your practice.

43
00:02:29,351 --> 00:02:31,793
[SPEAKER_02]: You recollect that you are responsible for your actions.

44
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:33,395
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that put together can know.

45
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:35,076
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what your mindfulness is.

46
00:02:35,476 --> 00:02:39,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like I recollect it and now I forget about it.

47
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:40,400
[SPEAKER_02]: If I ask you, can you...

48
00:02:41,703 --> 00:02:43,244
[SPEAKER_02]: Bring back to your mind, basically.

49
00:02:43,864 --> 00:02:54,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollect all the times, or rather, recollect how you felt all the times that you suffered in your life, on account of anything.

50
00:02:54,327 --> 00:02:55,628
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what specifically.

51
00:02:56,108 --> 00:02:57,868
[SPEAKER_02]: Just recollect the nature of suffering.

52
00:03:00,449 --> 00:03:05,831
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, go about your day with that recollection in your mind.

53
00:03:07,051 --> 00:03:11,413
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's nothing other than telling you how to go about your day being mindful of your feelings.

54
00:03:14,483 --> 00:03:18,946
[SPEAKER_01]: And does that recollection have to be in front of you?

55
00:03:19,386 --> 00:03:21,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just that it doesn't have to be.

56
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:23,188
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be in front of you.

57
00:03:23,308 --> 00:03:26,010
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're recollecting something that's not there.

58
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:34,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if, like, would you be recollecting now that you are, I don't know, clasping your hands together?

59
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:35,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'm recollecting this.

60
00:03:37,257 --> 00:03:37,417
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

61
00:03:37,597 --> 00:03:40,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollection is always on the level of the context of now.

62
00:03:40,859 --> 00:03:41,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

63
00:03:41,687 --> 00:03:45,128
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're doing your recollection now, means you're not seeing the context.

64
00:03:47,949 --> 00:03:52,151
[SPEAKER_02]: The context being the more... the general nature of things that you're supposed to discern.

65
00:03:52,311 --> 00:04:03,655
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in this particular instance, context being your body posture, and having hands, and having life, and not being dead, and all these other things, all these other things you can use to be mindful of.

66
00:04:04,835 --> 00:04:08,877
[SPEAKER_02]: So, being mindful of something doesn't mean attending and focusing on that thing.

67
00:04:09,517 --> 00:04:12,339
[SPEAKER_02]: means discerning the peripheral context in regard to that thing.

68
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Remembering the context.

69
00:04:17,902 --> 00:04:18,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Remembering.

70
00:04:20,663 --> 00:04:23,765
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to say remembering the context, because that implies you can remember something else.

71
00:04:23,945 --> 00:04:24,425
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you can't.

72
00:04:24,545 --> 00:04:25,646
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all you can remember.

73
00:04:27,705 --> 00:04:35,429
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all you can recollect is something that is to a degree known, as in experienced, discerned, maybe not fully understood.

74
00:04:36,269 --> 00:04:38,710
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, you know you're responsible for your actions.

75
00:04:39,350 --> 00:04:47,034
[SPEAKER_02]: It's when you forget that, basically, thing that you always are responsible for your actions, that's when you start acting irresponsibly.

76
00:04:48,075 --> 00:04:57,219
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you be acting irresponsibly with the thought in your mind resulting from directly, like, recollecting the weight of responsibility that you know you have?

77
00:04:57,827 --> 00:04:58,307
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't.

78
00:04:58,647 --> 00:04:59,128
[SPEAKER_02]: You couldn't.

79
00:05:00,568 --> 00:05:13,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Or would you be acting sensually towards sense desire, having established your mind properly, recollected the sign of repulsiveness, the inherent repulsiveness in everything beautiful, the inherent impermanence, the inherent danger.

80
00:05:14,854 --> 00:05:16,474
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are the thoughts you recollect.

81
00:05:17,475 --> 00:05:20,296
[SPEAKER_02]: But those thoughts are the context of your present situation.

82
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:28,634
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for as long as you think you can keep... you can be doing mindfulness in the present moment, means you are not mindful, because you are doing it.

83
00:05:29,694 --> 00:05:32,336
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not recollecting it, you're trying to basically perform it.

84
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:38,619
[SPEAKER_02]: And mindfulness and discernment is not on the level of performing.

85
00:05:38,779 --> 00:05:42,201
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it cannot be a method, it cannot be a technique, it cannot be a doing.

86
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:44,602
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it can free you from doing.

87
00:05:48,768 --> 00:05:54,391
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is a prescribed set of rules, you do this, you do this, you do this, and you're free from doing, that's a contradiction in terms.

88
00:05:56,252 --> 00:06:01,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, do good on account of doing bad, so stop doing bad, but do good, but it's still a form of doing.

89
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:03,195
[SPEAKER_02]: You're still bound by nature of action.

90
00:06:04,956 --> 00:06:08,638
[SPEAKER_02]: But discerning the context, discerning the nature of it, that's where the freedom is.

91
00:06:08,658 --> 00:06:16,322
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can't have too much of sati, and that's where sati results in discernment, in full understanding of nature of things.

92
00:06:18,763 --> 00:06:25,849
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why all society, recollectedness, results in samādhi, which is composure.

93
00:06:26,730 --> 00:06:37,358
[SPEAKER_02]: You are composed right here, right now, as a result of my fundamental, imperturbable, recollected context, that is still enduring, unchanged.

94
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:40,861
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like all the things you can be mindful of.

95
00:06:41,502 --> 00:06:45,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Your moods, the context, your feelings, the context of your actions.

96
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:45,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Death.

97
00:06:46,166 --> 00:06:47,146
[SPEAKER_02]: Death, the context.

98
00:06:47,226 --> 00:06:47,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

99
00:06:48,727 --> 00:06:51,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not being mindful of any old thing.

100
00:06:51,288 --> 00:06:56,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, you need to discern what it is.

101
00:06:56,391 --> 00:06:57,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

102
00:06:57,571 --> 00:07:04,655
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're actually practicing recollectedness for the sake of freedom from suffering, you need to know what you're supposed to recollect.

103
00:07:05,557 --> 00:07:10,420
[SPEAKER_02]: What you're supposed to bring back into your mind to endure is a context.

104
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:12,762
[SPEAKER_02]: And essentially a petitioner won't see that.

105
00:07:12,782 --> 00:07:15,944
[SPEAKER_02]: He cannot know that because he doesn't see the dhamma.

106
00:07:16,004 --> 00:07:18,626
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he doesn't know the way out of suffering.

107
00:07:19,006 --> 00:07:20,927
[SPEAKER_02]: How can he recollect the way out of suffering?

108
00:07:21,948 --> 00:07:25,010
[SPEAKER_02]: Having not found the way out of suffering, he cannot recollect it.

109
00:07:25,638 --> 00:07:29,080
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the mundane right view for Putujan is something he needs to strive for.

110
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:35,864
[SPEAKER_02]: There is responsibility, there is result of your actions, there is given, there are things that I don't understand and so on and so forth.

111
00:07:36,264 --> 00:07:40,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, taking it into account that you don't know and you acknowledge that.

112
00:07:42,435 --> 00:07:44,557
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's again, you recollect that context.

113
00:07:45,078 --> 00:07:50,002
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, if your mind is established upon the context of, oh, I don't know.

114
00:07:50,423 --> 00:07:52,364
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know the way out of suffering.

115
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:53,466
[SPEAKER_02]: And that remains.

116
00:07:53,626 --> 00:07:56,789
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't neglect that recollection.

117
00:07:58,210 --> 00:08:00,792
[SPEAKER_02]: When it sort of lapses, you bring back the recollection.

118
00:08:01,568 --> 00:08:04,510
[SPEAKER_02]: You re-clarify it, you re-discern it, so to speak.

119
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:13,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you then be able to take, leave, pick or choose or dismiss various instructions or the suttas you come across?

120
00:08:13,676 --> 00:08:14,136
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't.

121
00:08:15,977 --> 00:08:21,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you know that you don't know, which means you know that you don't have a reference point for right is right, wrong is wrong.

122
00:08:21,801 --> 00:08:26,584
[SPEAKER_02]: So you would take everything with a pinch of salt, but at the same time you wouldn't look for fault into things either.

123
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:33,004
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would be careful and maintain the context of one who doesn't know, who's trying to figure it out.

124
00:08:34,244 --> 00:08:45,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Or also, if you are maintaining the context of the one who doesn't know, would you then be able to, eh... forget about the fact that you don't know and that you're subjected to suffering and... and the whole... No, you wouldn't.

125
00:08:45,532 --> 00:08:48,334
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be... it would become a pressing task for you to do.

126
00:08:49,455 --> 00:08:53,318
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you don't necessarily dwell on the context that you don't know,

127
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:59,443
[SPEAKER_02]: You sort of drift, like, you know, if somebody were to, like, ask you, put you on the spot, you'll say, yes, I don't know.

128
00:08:59,924 --> 00:09:11,831
[SPEAKER_02]: But if nobody asks you, if you don't ask yourself that question, if you don't bring that recollection to your mind, you will drift away on account of things that you do, that make you feel better, that make you be a nicer person, and so on.

129
00:09:12,171 --> 00:09:15,494
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll end up being a patrujana who thinks he knows what practice of dhamma is.

130
00:09:16,174 --> 00:09:19,716
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's, like, the greatest obstruction you create for yourself.

131
00:09:19,776 --> 00:09:21,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Because then you won't look for what the right d

132
00:09:22,805 --> 00:09:25,526
[SPEAKER_02]: And the right dhamma begins when you know the way out of suffering.

133
00:09:26,447 --> 00:09:27,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Not before that.

134
00:09:28,608 --> 00:09:30,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, maybe you're doing it rightly, maybe you're doing it wrongly.

135
00:09:30,929 --> 00:09:39,653
[SPEAKER_02]: The context is, I still don't know, which means, I mustn't stop trying to find the right thing, until I know for sure.

136
00:09:39,734 --> 00:09:47,577
[SPEAKER_02]: And that kind of sense of urgency would come as a direct result of your correct or recollectiveness of the correct things.

137
00:09:49,659 --> 00:09:50,039
[SPEAKER_02]: But even

138
00:09:50,807 --> 00:09:57,696
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, even if you don't necessarily have any dhamma instruction, sati, mindfulness, is recollectedness.

139
00:09:59,479 --> 00:10:07,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, as Dīrūla gave the example, like that, oh, be mindful when you go into that kuti, because there is a slippery gutter there.

140
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,985
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now you go into the kuti, and you're opening the door carefully, and you're carefully putting your foot down, so you don't slip.

141
00:10:15,105 --> 00:10:18,348
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the second step, carefully... That's not what mindfulness is.

142
00:10:18,568 --> 00:10:19,509
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what sati is.

143
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:26,335
[SPEAKER_02]: All of those things you're doing carefully, are result of your sati, which is awareness of the context of danger.

144
00:10:28,397 --> 00:10:31,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the context of entering the kuti's potential injury.

145
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:35,263
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that, you are stepping in slowly.

146
00:10:36,643 --> 00:10:49,098
[SPEAKER_02]: But most people, probably due to the whole kind of notion of, you know, meditative techniques and whatnot, associate mindfulness with that kind of close attention, like staring at my feeling the soles of my foot.

147
00:10:49,178 --> 00:10:49,719
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm mindful.

148
00:10:49,739 --> 00:10:49,919
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

149
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:54,385
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't even do that unless somebody pointed out the possible danger of slipping.

150
00:10:57,426 --> 00:10:58,726
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's where the mindfulness is.

151
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:03,487
[SPEAKER_02]: In discerning the context of your present intentions that are leading you towards present actions.

152
00:11:04,108 --> 00:11:05,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Not towards, like, cancelling everything.

153
00:11:06,088 --> 00:11:09,989
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, don't think about the context, don't think about the fear, don't think about the danger.

154
00:11:10,269 --> 00:11:15,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Just touching, touching, moving, moving, opening, sitting, stepping, whatever else.

155
00:11:15,510 --> 00:11:17,551
[SPEAKER_02]: That's, like, completely the opposite of the context.

156
00:11:18,991 --> 00:11:22,272
[SPEAKER_02]: And, ironically, that's also done on account of the context.

157
00:11:22,332 --> 00:11:23,312
[SPEAKER_02]: Context of ignoring.

158
00:11:24,012 --> 00:11:26,873
[SPEAKER_02]: You value ignoring the context as a practice of dhamma.

159
00:11:28,908 --> 00:11:31,510
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why I want to commit and overly absorb into things.

160
00:11:32,110 --> 00:11:37,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Hopefully, that that's how you kind of pull yourself away from all the thoughts and feelings and moods that bother you.

161
00:11:37,574 --> 00:11:40,776
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, how many times you heard from, you know, the teachers?

162
00:11:41,237 --> 00:11:42,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, just stop thinking.

163
00:11:42,698 --> 00:11:44,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Or just place your awareness on the breath.

164
00:11:45,159 --> 00:11:49,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is, again, just just focus on breathing or do the mantra Buddha in your head.

165
00:11:49,762 --> 00:11:50,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Just don't think.

166
00:11:52,464 --> 00:11:55,346
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you even understand that sentence without thinking it?

167
00:11:56,927 --> 00:11:57,628
[SPEAKER_02]: So, here you are.

168
00:11:58,203 --> 00:11:59,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Thinking that you shouldn't be thinking.

169
00:12:00,944 --> 00:12:07,686
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, adhering to your thinking, the things that doesn't think, as the way of... out of thinking.

170
00:12:09,826 --> 00:12:12,327
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why always results in just frustration, basically.

171
00:12:13,387 --> 00:12:22,389
[SPEAKER_02]: And if people they are... if people who are engaging in this kind of practice say, no, I'm not frustrated, well, then you could also ask yourself, do you get upset when somebody says that your practice is wrong?

172
00:12:23,510 --> 00:12:24,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you get defensive?

173
00:12:27,796 --> 00:12:35,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if that sort of starts you off in a defensive direction, means, oh, so you're adhering to something blindly.

174
00:12:36,278 --> 00:12:40,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you being affected by coming across a view that says, no, that's wrong?

175
00:12:42,219 --> 00:12:43,599
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to take it personally.

176
00:12:43,639 --> 00:12:47,400
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you're doing it rightly, you will be unable to take it personally.

177
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:53,061
[SPEAKER_02]: So, all these little pointers, basically, but they're not seen because the whole practice is a practice of looking away from the context.

178
00:12:53,937 --> 00:13:00,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Ironically, at the pretext of mindfulness, which is basically recollectiveness of the context.

179
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:10,142
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can see, if the context is clearly discerned, and it's enduring there, presently now, you're responsible for your actions.

180
00:13:10,762 --> 00:13:17,184
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you are, you will be acting, whatever you now choose to do within that context, you will be acting in full awareness.

181
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:21,345
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the context is clear and imperturbable.

182
00:13:23,132 --> 00:13:30,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you then act in lack of awareness and do something irresponsible, while the context of responsibility is there, enduring, and you're feeling it?

183
00:13:30,977 --> 00:13:31,358
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't.

184
00:13:31,418 --> 00:13:31,958
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

185
00:13:32,599 --> 00:13:38,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, sati, sampajañña, mindfulness, recollectedness and awareness.

186
00:13:44,487 --> 00:13:45,768
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't do mindfulness.

187
00:13:46,308 --> 00:13:46,868
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

188
00:13:47,129 --> 00:13:48,970
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're doing it, means that's not mindfulness.

189
00:13:51,732 --> 00:13:55,693
[SPEAKER_02]: What you're doing is the result of recollectedness or lack of it.

190
00:13:57,693 --> 00:14:01,254
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the, you know, the right view comes first, even for the right mindfulness.

191
00:14:01,374 --> 00:14:05,595
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't say samāsati comes first and then samādhiti comes first, second.

192
00:14:05,795 --> 00:14:05,955
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

193
00:14:06,995 --> 00:14:15,437
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the right view that then, ah, now you know what you're supposed to recollect and now your recollectedness will be samāsati.

194
00:14:19,602 --> 00:14:22,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do a right technique, that will then result in the right view.

195
00:14:22,624 --> 00:14:23,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

196
00:14:24,745 --> 00:14:31,389
[SPEAKER_02]: So, having developed right view, having discerned the right view, your recollectedness will be right.

197
00:14:32,490 --> 00:14:38,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Having discerned the right view, having right recollectedness enduring, your composure will be right.

198
00:14:40,694 --> 00:14:41,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Saṅgha, samādhi.

199
00:14:46,214 --> 00:14:56,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having recognized the context, and remembering the context... Yeah.

200
00:14:57,684 --> 00:15:03,028
[SPEAKER_02]: And see, remembering the context, people might say, ah, so I just have to keep telling to myself what the context is.

201
00:15:04,349 --> 00:15:05,711
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not remembering the context.

202
00:15:05,731 --> 00:15:08,193
[SPEAKER_02]: That's you just basically trying to do it again.

203
00:15:10,005 --> 00:15:12,207
[SPEAKER_02]: So, remembering the context means recollect.

204
00:15:12,267 --> 00:15:13,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you responsible for your actions?

205
00:15:13,688 --> 00:15:15,489
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, like, whatever we always do.

206
00:15:15,770 --> 00:15:17,591
[SPEAKER_02]: What is your present enduring mood right now?

207
00:15:18,432 --> 00:15:19,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Positive, negative, neutral?

208
00:15:21,254 --> 00:15:23,596
[SPEAKER_02]: See, you recollect what's already there.

209
00:15:23,896 --> 00:15:26,038
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not creating the mood by giving me the answer.

210
00:15:26,638 --> 00:15:28,119
[SPEAKER_02]: You just...

211
00:15:28,139 --> 00:15:35,005
[SPEAKER_02]: The question directed you in the direction of the context, which is the present enduring pleasant mood.

212
00:15:35,705 --> 00:15:36,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

213
00:15:36,991 --> 00:15:40,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you don't have to keep saying, I'm in a good mood, good mood, good mood.

214
00:15:41,294 --> 00:15:43,536
[SPEAKER_02]: That will distract you from present-enduring mood.

215
00:15:46,038 --> 00:15:48,880
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now you just have to remain mindful of it peripherally.

216
00:15:49,020 --> 00:15:49,821
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole point.

217
00:15:49,841 --> 00:15:50,781
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what mindfulness is.

218
00:15:50,941 --> 00:15:52,222
[SPEAKER_02]: That peripheral discernment.

219
00:15:53,784 --> 00:15:56,886
[SPEAKER_02]: Discernment of the container, of the foundation.

220
00:15:57,326 --> 00:16:05,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Would it be like, if you said, remembering or... recollecting, recollecting it.

221
00:16:08,635 --> 00:16:17,618
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not so much as something that you do, but not not engaging in anything that contradicts it.

222
00:16:18,138 --> 00:16:19,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Protecting what you recollected.

223
00:16:19,739 --> 00:16:19,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

224
00:16:20,539 --> 00:16:30,582
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what Guru would say, that everybody misjudges, that you guard your mind, like the mother guards her own child.

225
00:16:32,962 --> 00:16:35,604
[SPEAKER_02]: With that state of mind, you will cherish all living beings.

226
00:16:36,665 --> 00:16:48,817
[SPEAKER_02]: But, I mean, this is like unrelated, but the funny thing is, the way the chanting is done and translated, it sounds as if you will basically, like the mother guards her own child, you cherish all living beings.

227
00:16:50,158 --> 00:16:54,802
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point is to recollect the mind correctly, protect the mind, as in protect the wholesome states.

228
00:16:56,944 --> 00:17:01,068
[SPEAKER_02]: Make sure there is no arising of unwholesome and no cessation of wholesome.

229
00:17:01,228 --> 00:17:07,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you engage in something, if you choose to engage in something unwholesome, then you're basically preventing yourself from recollecting.

230
00:17:08,094 --> 00:17:17,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So you're practicing mindfulness by knowing what you're supposed to recollect, which is already a form of recollecting it, and then protecting that which is enduring through that recollection.

231
00:17:17,782 --> 00:17:18,803
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is your state of mind.

232
00:17:20,871 --> 00:17:22,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you recollected it.

233
00:17:23,353 --> 00:17:24,414
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do now?

234
00:17:25,115 --> 00:17:27,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Let it endure without distracting yourself from it.

235
00:17:28,678 --> 00:17:31,541
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you... and that's what we spoke in the previous talk.

236
00:17:32,081 --> 00:17:37,146
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't lose your recollectedness accidentally.

237
00:17:37,746 --> 00:17:45,293
[SPEAKER_02]: You only lose it when you choose to basically act out of desire, aversion or desire for distraction.

238
00:17:46,706 --> 00:17:53,214
[SPEAKER_02]: So, through your actions, you basically erode the context that you now need to re-recollect.

239
00:17:54,155 --> 00:17:54,916
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can still do it.

240
00:17:54,936 --> 00:18:02,145
[SPEAKER_02]: You re-recollect it, then your actions are seen as discrepant to the context that now you value like a mother values her only child.

241
00:18:05,055 --> 00:18:16,365
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you will abstain from the actions and it will be... it won't require effort and... and like, oh, I must say no, I must say no, because you value the context and these actions are incompatible with the context.

242
00:18:16,826 --> 00:18:24,953
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you can then engage with these actions is if you stop recollecting, stop or lose the... what you recollected of that context.

243
00:18:25,694 --> 00:18:26,455
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will lose it

244
00:18:27,290 --> 00:18:31,132
[SPEAKER_02]: When you carelessly take it for granted, start looking away from it, start distracting.

245
00:18:31,192 --> 00:18:31,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this is fine.

246
00:18:31,912 --> 00:18:34,993
[SPEAKER_02]: This won't take me away from my recollectedness.

247
00:18:35,914 --> 00:18:38,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Next thing you know, you lost what you recollected.

248
00:18:38,294 --> 00:18:39,315
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to redo it.

249
00:18:44,897 --> 00:18:54,241
[SPEAKER_00]: But one who has recognized that what needs to be recollected... Then, you just maintain that recollection in being, which is the definition of bhāvanā.

250
00:18:55,728 --> 00:19:00,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Maintaining it, like, like, protect it while it's enduring there on its own, because that's the context you, you discern.

251
00:19:01,473 --> 00:19:12,403
[SPEAKER_02]: And as a result of you protecting the context now that's, that's being there enduring, as your state of mind, everything incompatible to that context is being sort of, you know, burnt away.

252
00:19:13,544 --> 00:19:19,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like you've understood something, and now you're not doing anything that's gonna confuse that.

253
00:19:20,030 --> 00:19:21,751
[SPEAKER_02]: It's gonna cover it up, distract you from it.

254
00:19:22,172 --> 00:19:22,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

255
00:19:26,653 --> 00:19:27,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness.

256
00:19:27,473 --> 00:19:28,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of breathing.

257
00:19:28,374 --> 00:19:29,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Recollection of breathing.

258
00:19:30,335 --> 00:19:30,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

259
00:19:30,655 --> 00:19:32,336
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollectedness of breathing.

260
00:19:32,616 --> 00:19:34,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

261
00:19:34,197 --> 00:19:35,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollectedness of breathing.

262
00:19:35,277 --> 00:19:48,444
[SPEAKER_02]: So... Well, that's... as we already clarified in a number of talks, okay, on peripheral awareness, we've done the whole talk on mindfulness of breath, basically, discernment of the context that endures while you're doing this, while you're doing that, and so on.

263
00:19:49,164 --> 00:19:53,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, now, in the light of the recent talk of recollectedness, being the word for sati,

264
00:19:56,579 --> 00:20:02,622
[SPEAKER_02]: You read the Sutta in itself, Ānāpānasati Sutta, and it already states that.

265
00:20:02,642 --> 00:20:10,546
[SPEAKER_02]: It states first that the audience of the Ānāpānasati Sutta were at least Sotāpannas, which means they were all of the right view.

266
00:20:10,906 --> 00:20:12,427
[SPEAKER_02]: So now they have the right mindfulness.

267
00:20:13,272 --> 00:20:22,894
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, there are different loads of ways of right mindfulness, but in this particular sutra, the way of the right, sorry, recollectedness, is the recollectedness of breathing.

268
00:20:22,914 --> 00:20:26,295
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's that's how the the actual instruction starts.

269
00:20:27,495 --> 00:20:32,276
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing in long, as in there is breathing in long, he knows he's breathing in long.

270
00:20:33,576 --> 00:20:37,157
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like, I am breathing in long and I know I'm breathing in long.

271
00:20:37,577 --> 00:20:42,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing in long, while breathing in long, he knows there is a breathing in long.

272
00:20:43,725 --> 00:20:50,110
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recollect the most obvious, the most apparent, the most neutral knowledge of what's already there.

273
00:20:53,712 --> 00:21:00,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you recollect the breath, if the breath is not there?

274
00:21:00,677 --> 00:21:01,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

275
00:21:02,899 --> 00:21:05,641
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's important order here to maintain, because

276
00:21:06,351 --> 00:21:14,453
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, people would even read that sutta, which is very clear, but, yeah, I'm sure people say, well, it's very clear that's an instruction, and people take it to be an instruction.

277
00:21:14,993 --> 00:21:15,393
[SPEAKER_02]: But he doesn't.

278
00:21:15,413 --> 00:21:16,333
[SPEAKER_02]: He says he knows.

279
00:21:16,933 --> 00:21:18,734
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't say he does the breath like this.

280
00:21:19,434 --> 00:21:22,895
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, while breathing like this, he knows that's what... that's how he's breathing.

281
00:21:24,235 --> 00:21:25,535
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows he's breathing long.

282
00:21:25,615 --> 00:21:26,235
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows.

283
00:21:27,096 --> 00:21:28,256
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can you do the knowledge?

284
00:21:29,216 --> 00:21:29,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, do the knowledge.

285
00:21:30,176 --> 00:21:30,996
[SPEAKER_02]: Do the knowledge for me.

286
00:21:31,737 --> 00:21:33,097
[SPEAKER_02]: Do the method of knowledge.

287
00:21:34,553 --> 00:21:35,674
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only know what's there.

288
00:21:35,694 --> 00:21:36,314
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only know.

289
00:21:36,354 --> 00:21:39,837
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why knowledge results from correct recollectedness.

290
00:21:40,357 --> 00:21:42,378
[SPEAKER_02]: You get to know what you keep recollecting.

291
00:21:44,059 --> 00:21:54,446
[SPEAKER_02]: And the only reason, by the way, you can then practice whatever, focusing on your nostrils or other breath, is because of the context of the breathing is there, that obviously now you're not mindful of.

292
00:21:57,168 --> 00:21:58,689
[SPEAKER_02]: You lost the recollection of it.

293
00:21:59,209 --> 00:22:03,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you don't need to be thinking of your nostrils in order to know that you are breathing.

294
00:22:04,512 --> 00:22:10,614
[SPEAKER_02]: All you need to do is don't distract yourself from your knowledge that you are already breathing.

295
00:22:10,814 --> 00:22:12,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see how you're breathing now.

296
00:22:12,375 --> 00:22:14,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

297
00:22:14,916 --> 00:22:18,017
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to start your knowledge by the first...

298
00:22:19,058 --> 00:22:21,079
[SPEAKER_02]: This is how I'm supposed to be breathing.

299
00:22:21,119 --> 00:22:22,399
[SPEAKER_02]: You know it's the long breath, okay.

300
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:25,221
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now I've got a short breath at the moment.

301
00:22:25,241 --> 00:22:26,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have to wait for the long breath.

302
00:22:26,421 --> 00:22:26,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

303
00:22:26,961 --> 00:22:30,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Then... No, you've got to do the long breathing first, and then...

304
00:22:30,343 --> 00:22:34,744
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you start, you start the mindfulness of breathing by asking yourself, am I breathing right now?

305
00:22:37,165 --> 00:22:37,785
[SPEAKER_02]: The answer is yes.

306
00:22:39,286 --> 00:22:41,367
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the breathing while I'm breathing?

307
00:22:43,795 --> 00:22:46,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, breathing is in, breathing is out.

308
00:22:46,416 --> 00:22:49,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing is bodily in, of air, bodily out, of air.

309
00:22:50,197 --> 00:22:50,877
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the breathing.

310
00:22:51,077 --> 00:22:54,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And I know I'm breathing, because breathing is there, while I'm thinking of it.

311
00:22:55,438 --> 00:22:57,098
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the beginning of mindfulness of breath.

312
00:22:57,458 --> 00:23:04,760
[SPEAKER_02]: When you get used to that context, then, yeah, you can see, oh, now there is this kind of breathing in, and this kind of breathing out, that you know, not that you do.

313
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:07,100
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like knowing the mind, as we spoke.

314
00:23:07,140 --> 00:23:10,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, of course.

315
00:23:10,201 --> 00:23:11,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing the context, exactly.

316
00:23:12,462 --> 00:23:12,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like,

317
00:23:13,942 --> 00:23:26,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, there's something there that you... you're kind of, ehm... You can suddenly know something.

318
00:23:26,030 --> 00:23:26,730
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on its own.

319
00:23:26,790 --> 00:23:27,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

320
00:23:27,591 --> 00:23:28,771
[SPEAKER_01]: You only say and you know it.

321
00:23:28,931 --> 00:23:29,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You either recollect it.

322
00:23:29,912 --> 00:23:30,412
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be there.

323
00:23:30,452 --> 00:23:30,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

324
00:23:31,333 --> 00:23:32,834
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I meant.

325
00:23:32,994 --> 00:23:34,335
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't actually know if I finished the sentence.

326
00:23:34,735 --> 00:23:35,896
[SPEAKER_02]: But yeah, good point.

327
00:23:36,376 --> 00:23:37,897
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I said it's important order.

328
00:23:38,337 --> 00:23:39,278
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you...

329
00:23:41,227 --> 00:23:55,774
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't discern that, or if you forget that, you will keep trying to repossess your breathing, to regain the control of your breathing, which is completely the opposite of recognizing it being there on its own, independent of you.

330
00:23:56,508 --> 00:24:04,513
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're taking away that which you can't take away, which is the independence of the form, of the body, of the feelings, and trying to re-control.

331
00:24:04,613 --> 00:24:12,617
[SPEAKER_02]: And, again, that's why most people's meditation is meditation with centrality, which is meditation with control, with desire, with like this, with like that.

332
00:24:15,739 --> 00:24:20,842
[SPEAKER_00]: It's recognizing what the breath is, in that context.

333
00:24:21,582 --> 00:24:26,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognizing what currently... what breath currently is enduring on its own.

334
00:24:28,111 --> 00:24:33,055
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's the verb, actually.

335
00:24:33,096 --> 00:24:39,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I would tend to avoid saying recollection of the breath, because it's something that you're doing.

336
00:24:39,802 --> 00:24:42,124
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something that's... it's an intention.

337
00:24:42,284 --> 00:24:45,467
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like... so, it's actually the verb breathing in.

338
00:24:46,368 --> 00:24:49,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's recollecting breathing in, rather than...

339
00:24:51,022 --> 00:24:57,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, just for me, the breath, when I say recollection of the breath, or mindfulness of the breath, people think, oh, it's this...

340
00:24:59,089 --> 00:25:00,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Being present while I'm doing this.

341
00:25:00,610 --> 00:25:02,051
[SPEAKER_01]: This thing that I focus on.

342
00:25:02,131 --> 00:25:03,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

343
00:25:03,572 --> 00:25:05,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Focus on this while I'm doing it.

344
00:25:05,093 --> 00:25:05,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

345
00:25:06,474 --> 00:25:08,996
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's, yeah, that's what I mean, like, breathing in, breathing out, it's there.

346
00:25:09,997 --> 00:25:13,060
[SPEAKER_02]: They're bound with the body, they're bodily, they're being done by the body.

347
00:25:14,241 --> 00:25:17,223
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you are discerning that.

348
00:25:18,382 --> 00:25:21,845
[SPEAKER_02]: So, breathing in long, he knows breathing in is long.

349
00:25:23,647 --> 00:25:24,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, I am breathing in.

350
00:25:25,828 --> 00:25:28,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Later on, and that's that's what you got your phone out.

351
00:25:28,871 --> 00:25:29,512
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I thought.

352
00:25:29,552 --> 00:25:30,893
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to look at the Pali as well.

353
00:25:31,153 --> 00:25:31,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

354
00:25:32,074 --> 00:25:32,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

355
00:25:32,334 --> 00:25:37,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first few are breathing in long.

356
00:25:38,139 --> 00:25:38,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I am breathing...

357
00:25:38,760 --> 00:25:40,061
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows I am breathing in long.

358
00:25:40,081 --> 00:25:40,221
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

359
00:25:41,182 --> 00:25:42,643
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he knows I am breathing in long.

360
00:25:43,123 --> 00:25:44,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing in long, he knows I'm breathing in long.

361
00:25:45,672 --> 00:25:51,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you could say, like, having breathing in short, enduring, he knows I am breathing in short.

362
00:25:51,274 --> 00:25:51,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

363
00:26:07,992 --> 00:26:12,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Having breathing out long, enduring, he knows I am breathing out long.

364
00:26:13,053 --> 00:26:13,974
[SPEAKER_02]: There's this thing there.

365
00:26:14,534 --> 00:26:17,835
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that, I am breathing like this or I am breathing like that.

366
00:26:17,895 --> 00:26:20,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because I am breathing like this or that.

367
00:26:21,836 --> 00:26:22,836
[SPEAKER_01]: I is secondary to it.

368
00:26:24,576 --> 00:26:30,198
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, I is basically just an expression as in I know that because of this breath, this is the breath here.

369
00:26:33,959 --> 00:26:35,400
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something there and I know it's there.

370
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:35,780
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

371
00:26:36,721 --> 00:26:37,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And the body is there.

372
00:26:38,282 --> 00:26:45,126
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's why ānāpānasati, the sutta doesn't say it results in jhānas and ecstatic experiences.

373
00:26:45,547 --> 00:26:48,629
[SPEAKER_02]: It results in fulfillment of the four foundations of mindfulness.

374
00:26:48,929 --> 00:26:49,349
