567
00:39:32,781 --> 00:39:41,067
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same manner, you are subjected to this doing of this breathing, which is kind of... Another reason why breathing is a good thing to do is, is something

568
00:39:41,919 --> 00:39:43,960
[SPEAKER_02]: Everybody would regard it as the most personal thing.

569
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:49,122
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I was going to ask, why, why is it... Why is the breathing the good object?

570
00:39:49,763 --> 00:39:50,183
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why.

571
00:39:50,363 --> 00:39:53,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's the most personal type of action, seemingly.

572
00:39:53,644 --> 00:40:01,808
[SPEAKER_02]: But you realize that the most personal type of action, the most internal, the most inseparable, the most unoptional type of action, you are subjected to.

573
00:40:02,248 --> 00:40:02,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

574
00:40:03,149 --> 00:40:04,449
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's also, that's why it's neutral.

575
00:40:05,310 --> 00:40:07,451
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to have desire or aversion.

576
00:40:07,471 --> 00:40:08,331
[SPEAKER_02]: You will be breathing.

577
00:40:09,132 --> 00:40:09,912
[SPEAKER_02]: So it precedes

578
00:40:10,907 --> 00:40:13,548
[SPEAKER_02]: All the other types of actions that your mind usually depends on.

579
00:40:13,968 --> 00:40:18,210
[SPEAKER_01]: And isn't it also like, like, your bodily posture is something that you're always doing?

580
00:40:18,290 --> 00:40:32,036
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, eh... No, but the reason why it's, why it's a, it's a good thing to, like, develop the context in regard to, as in, something of it, is because it's neutral, yet it contains the nature of the action.

581
00:40:32,596 --> 00:40:34,757
[SPEAKER_02]: It is an action, or at least that's the view.

582
00:40:36,146 --> 00:40:41,629
[SPEAKER_02]: So, while... like, can you be... can you breathe in long, if the body is not breathing in long?

583
00:40:42,729 --> 00:40:42,950
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't.

584
00:40:43,570 --> 00:40:49,593
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even when you say, I am breathing in long, the only reason you can assume the possession of it, is because the body is doing it.

585
00:40:49,613 --> 00:40:54,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you notice that there's something there, which is the body breathing in.

586
00:40:55,856 --> 00:41:01,942
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in the same sense as we described in other talks, I don't really know the order I'm uploading them, but people will come across it.

587
00:41:02,342 --> 00:41:06,526
[SPEAKER_02]: We said, like, the body is the representative of the only matter that you're exposed to.

588
00:41:06,546 --> 00:41:08,528
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the... your body is your universe.

589
00:41:08,888 --> 00:41:14,654
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you try to, like, repossess the matter, the matter, rūpa, it's always a step below your feet.

590
00:41:15,915 --> 00:41:17,437
[SPEAKER_02]: The distance always remains the same.

591
00:41:17,477 --> 00:41:18,798
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot possibly enter the

592
00:41:19,346 --> 00:41:20,847
[SPEAKER_02]: The breath is the same.

593
00:41:20,907 --> 00:41:24,309
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter how slow, how controllably you're doing your breath, you are within it.

594
00:41:24,389 --> 00:41:24,529
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

595
00:41:35,077 --> 00:41:36,058
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not within you.

596
00:41:36,178 --> 00:41:36,878
[SPEAKER_01]: The breathing, yeah.

597
00:41:36,898 --> 00:41:37,819
[SPEAKER_01]: You are within the breathing.

598
00:41:37,839 --> 00:41:39,300
[SPEAKER_02]: There's always this... Because you're within the body.

599
00:41:39,320 --> 00:41:39,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

600
00:41:40,141 --> 00:41:44,964
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Buddha taught it, instead of, you know, another mindfulness of death.

601
00:41:45,644 --> 00:41:48,186
[SPEAKER_01]: I, I kind of... that's like... it's the same wording, by the way.

602
00:41:48,206 --> 00:41:50,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same wording for the bodily postures.

603
00:41:50,468 --> 00:41:51,268
[SPEAKER_01]: It's exactly the same.

604
00:41:51,288 --> 00:41:52,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sitting down... Yeah, you're within.

605
00:41:53,049 --> 00:41:55,391
[SPEAKER_01]: One understands, I'm sitting down.

606
00:41:55,711 --> 00:41:58,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing in, he understands, I'm breathing in.

607
00:41:58,735 --> 00:41:59,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure.

608
00:41:59,556 --> 00:42:00,697
[SPEAKER_02]: It's satipaṭṭhāna.

609
00:42:00,818 --> 00:42:03,961
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the foundations of recollectedness.

610
00:42:04,502 --> 00:42:11,310
[SPEAKER_02]: But in another sutta, when we've done the mindfulness, the breathing toward death, it was when monks were practicing maraṇasati,

611
00:42:12,964 --> 00:42:17,789
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, yeah, he got a bit, got a bit edgy, and so on.

612
00:42:17,869 --> 00:42:19,811
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so they were committing suicide, and so on.

613
00:42:19,831 --> 00:42:25,377
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, they were, instead of that, yeah, but for the same result, practice the breathing.

614
00:42:25,858 --> 00:42:26,999
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't jump off cliffs, then?

615
00:42:27,139 --> 00:42:33,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but it's still, it's going to develop the same mindfulness of the same death, being the same container you cannot step outside of.

616
00:42:33,666 --> 00:42:34,187
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

617
00:42:36,225 --> 00:42:37,786
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, you don't notice your breath.

618
00:42:38,267 --> 00:42:47,312
[SPEAKER_02]: You might discern that your body is your container, your senses are your container, the world is a container, my possessions are my container, and so on, depending how undeveloped your mind is.

619
00:42:47,952 --> 00:42:53,575
[SPEAKER_02]: But, fundamentally, the most immediate container is the image of the body of breathing in, breathing out.

620
00:42:54,075 --> 00:42:57,757
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's much closer to everything else you do while you're breathing.

621
00:42:58,578 --> 00:43:00,039
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is really no closer to that.

622
00:43:00,059 --> 00:43:02,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, mindfulness of death is closer to that.

623
00:43:04,052 --> 00:43:19,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, utter removal of anything, anything, any necessary basis for your experience as a whole, that's why it can result quickly in full uprooting of this patient, or it can tip you over a bit, too quickly.

624
00:43:19,217 --> 00:43:24,179
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not as close as mindfulness to death, but there is nothing closer, except that.

625
00:43:25,284 --> 00:43:28,865
[SPEAKER_02]: To the point of you developing in terms of where you're enclosed within.

626
00:43:29,085 --> 00:43:30,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you imagine being outside?

627
00:43:31,106 --> 00:43:31,226
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

628
00:43:31,366 --> 00:43:34,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you imagine doing anything without a breathing body?

629
00:43:34,687 --> 00:43:43,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, even if, like, there is... you're holding your breathing, it's only done on account of the breathing body, not delaying the breathing of the breathing body.

630
00:43:45,818 --> 00:43:48,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how can you be holding it if it's not there?

631
00:43:48,180 --> 00:43:50,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, the intention to breathe is still there.

632
00:43:50,262 --> 00:43:51,403
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... Of course.

633
00:43:51,643 --> 00:43:53,164
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't take a step outside of that.

634
00:43:53,204 --> 00:43:54,745
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of breath is still there.

635
00:43:55,346 --> 00:43:56,607
[SPEAKER_02]: The action of breathing is still there.

636
00:44:07,924 --> 00:44:10,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, what about the heart, you know, the beating of heart?

637
00:44:11,646 --> 00:44:15,908
[SPEAKER_00]: I breathe, I beat in quick, I beat in slow.

638
00:44:17,409 --> 00:44:21,712
[SPEAKER_02]: No, see, you can contemplate that, but that would be more on the level of maraṇasati.

639
00:44:22,052 --> 00:44:22,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

640
00:44:22,932 --> 00:44:27,455
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you could see how you could just fill somebody with anxiety, if you recognize it.

641
00:44:27,615 --> 00:44:32,818
[SPEAKER_02]: Literally, if you do see a connection, how entire, your entire life of joys...

642
00:44:34,092 --> 00:44:43,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Your entire life of joys and miseries, of everything, things that are important, your value, possessions, everything, literally depends on this random beat that you have.

643
00:44:44,054 --> 00:44:45,894
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even conceive controlling.

644
00:44:45,954 --> 00:44:50,555
[SPEAKER_02]: You can conceive controlling breath, which is why it's a good converging point to undo that conceiving.

645
00:44:52,195 --> 00:44:59,776
[SPEAKER_02]: This will just cut through your own conceivings, and if the mind is strong enough, that cutting through would result in, like, yeah, full dispassion and arahantship.

646
00:45:00,136 --> 00:45:03,117
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind is not strong enough, well, that cutting through might cut you.

647
00:45:04,080 --> 00:45:06,061
[SPEAKER_01]: As it did to a certain number of monks.

648
00:45:06,681 --> 00:45:09,102
[SPEAKER_02]: When they were jumping off the cliffs and whatnot, they couldn't handle it.

649
00:45:12,644 --> 00:45:16,606
[SPEAKER_02]: But breathing, like, there is an assumption of my involvement with it.

650
00:45:17,758 --> 00:45:22,622
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that I'm just so randomly subjected to an utter, utter lack of control.

651
00:45:23,382 --> 00:45:41,015
[SPEAKER_02]: But that... that's... that any sense of control you have over your breathing is undercut by... Is within the breathing that you are subjected to, so that you realize my sense of control is basically not... cannot become primordial, cannot take the possession of its necessary basis for my control.

652
00:45:41,075 --> 00:45:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Even something as basic as breathing.

653
00:45:42,957 --> 00:45:43,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

654
00:45:44,242 --> 00:45:45,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a neutral...

655
00:45:45,843 --> 00:45:47,183
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a neutral action, yeah.

656
00:45:47,203 --> 00:45:50,745
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it will not result necessarily in destroying this disgusting...

657
00:45:50,765 --> 00:45:51,425
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just breathing.

658
00:45:51,866 --> 00:45:54,527
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, I'm fully enclosed within this just breathing.

659
00:45:54,687 --> 00:45:56,127
[SPEAKER_02]: I cannot step outside of this breathing.

660
00:45:58,529 --> 00:46:11,314
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you stop maintaining the assumption of ownership, stepping outside, as we discussed that before, the assumption of external in regard to this breathing, what is then to be said about all the other even more proliferate assumptions?

661
00:46:11,495 --> 00:46:12,295
[SPEAKER_02]: They will be abandoned.

662
00:46:15,735 --> 00:46:18,936
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you think your body is here, that's why you're breathing.

663
00:46:18,956 --> 00:46:22,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Your notion of body is here, because your breathing is there.

664
00:46:23,877 --> 00:46:27,698
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you choose to act on account of your body, that's even further down the line.

665
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:38,521
[SPEAKER_02]: You're subjected to breathing, that's why you know the body.

666
00:46:41,587 --> 00:46:42,727
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I know... Yeah.

667
00:46:43,088 --> 00:46:49,390
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can relate to the body, that's why you can possess the body, that's why you can chase the sense objects to the body, for the body, and so on.

668
00:46:50,370 --> 00:46:51,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you could say you're sitting down.

669
00:46:52,451 --> 00:46:53,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you know it's the body.

670
00:46:54,531 --> 00:46:55,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you not say that as well?

671
00:46:55,491 --> 00:46:56,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but now you're talking about a different...

672
00:46:56,912 --> 00:47:00,633
[SPEAKER_01]: I know, but just, like, it's not like, yeah, it's not the only way you can know the body.

673
00:47:00,753 --> 00:47:02,154
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we're talking about mindfulness of breathing.

674
00:47:02,294 --> 00:47:02,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

675
00:47:02,994 --> 00:47:05,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You can know the body through the postures that the body has been subjected to.

676
00:47:08,523 --> 00:47:11,625
[SPEAKER_02]: You can know the body through contemplating death of the body.

677
00:47:11,665 --> 00:47:11,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

678
00:47:15,587 --> 00:47:16,987
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why these things are contemplations.

679
00:47:19,389 --> 00:47:25,252
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why sati, recollectiveness, results in full understanding, in wisdom, in discernment, whatever you want to call it.

680
00:47:27,053 --> 00:47:27,753
[SPEAKER_02]: You see it directly, yeah.

681
00:47:27,833 --> 00:47:29,914
[SPEAKER_02]: If you keep recollecting it, you become more familiar with it.

682
00:47:30,054 --> 00:47:35,657
[SPEAKER_02]: More familiar with it, the more signs and tokens and features of it become discerned, the more it gets established.

683
00:47:40,293 --> 00:47:45,694
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a technique, which there are many out there, to calm your body, to give you energy or whatever.

684
00:47:46,154 --> 00:47:56,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You have fear, so you breathe in, you don't breathe, you breathe in deep, you breathe in shallow, whatever, to manage a certain experience you're having, that you don't want to be.

685
00:47:56,856 --> 00:47:59,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear, anger, excitement, you want to be calm.

686
00:48:00,677 --> 00:48:07,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those things are... they work, but that is not... you don't have the knowledge

687
00:48:08,932 --> 00:48:10,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

688
00:48:13,215 --> 00:48:14,776
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they don't work.

689
00:48:15,497 --> 00:48:17,158
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to define what working is.

690
00:48:17,178 --> 00:48:20,781
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say, oh, it helps me, thus it works.

691
00:48:21,261 --> 00:48:25,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Working means it frees you from suffering, frees you from need of management.

692
00:48:25,825 --> 00:48:32,950
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, if things are working, they will make you unable to be affected by issues in the first place.

693
00:48:33,790 --> 00:48:35,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just they will perfectly manage the issues.

694
00:48:37,306 --> 00:48:40,548
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if things are not working on that level, then things are not working.

695
00:48:41,248 --> 00:48:49,833
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if your working is on the level of management, you shouldn't call it the same working as the level of uprooting that which I'm managing now.

696
00:48:49,853 --> 00:48:52,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you're not affected in the first place, there is nothing for you to manage.

697
00:48:54,695 --> 00:49:03,500
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's often that's kept intentionally, that blur between, well, maybe it doesn't work for us to be a sotāpanna, but it still works.

698
00:49:03,560 --> 00:49:05,221
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, how do you know unless you're a sotāpanna?

699
00:49:05,681 --> 00:49:06,702
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then it doesn't work.

700
00:49:09,919 --> 00:49:22,828
[SPEAKER_02]: But yes, so, the management, when it's used as management, it can help manage the pain, but that's, again, or manage the difficult, manage the issues, but all of those things are secondary to the fact that the issues are first.

701
00:49:23,488 --> 00:49:26,210
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the thing, the word manage, oh, it implies something's wrong.

702
00:49:26,882 --> 00:49:27,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

703
00:49:27,703 --> 00:49:28,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you manage.

704
00:49:28,844 --> 00:49:31,005
[SPEAKER_02]: You require a problem in order to manage it.

705
00:49:31,245 --> 00:49:33,747
[SPEAKER_02]: But the dhamma doesn't manage the problems, the dhamma uproots them.

706
00:49:34,308 --> 00:49:40,232
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindfulness of breathing, the Buddha said, if you have the right view, will result in arahantship, if you cultivate it and make much of it.

707
00:49:41,513 --> 00:49:44,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Means it's about uprooting, not about perfect management.

708
00:49:44,516 --> 00:49:48,259
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about making you unable to be affected by that which you used to have to manage.

709
00:49:54,043 --> 00:49:55,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from management.

710
00:49:55,991 --> 00:49:56,171
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

711
00:49:56,271 --> 00:49:59,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Freedom from burden.

712
00:49:59,072 --> 00:49:59,972
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a form of burden.

713
00:50:00,052 --> 00:50:04,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Having to do something on account of what you felt, on account of what touched you, what contacted you.

714
00:50:05,653 --> 00:50:06,233
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to do it.

715
00:50:06,853 --> 00:50:07,853
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't help by doing it.

716
00:50:09,434 --> 00:50:10,014
[SPEAKER_02]: You're burdened.

717
00:50:11,194 --> 00:50:11,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Pressure.

718
00:50:12,434 --> 00:50:13,435
[SPEAKER_02]: You're free from all of that.

719
00:50:16,975 --> 00:50:23,637
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

720
00:50:23,717 --> 00:50:24,937
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're doing it as a technique,

721
00:50:25,758 --> 00:50:31,381
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot result in the right context, because your current context of doing it as a technique is the context of management.

722
00:50:32,942 --> 00:50:33,142
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

723
00:50:33,342 --> 00:50:36,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you want to do meditational breathing?

724
00:50:36,804 --> 00:50:41,447
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're bothered by something, or because you want something that you don't have, which is what bothers you.

725
00:50:41,547 --> 00:50:42,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's your context.

726
00:50:43,048 --> 00:50:45,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you fully understanding and discerning that context?

727
00:50:49,671 --> 00:50:51,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Through you watching your nostrils.

728
00:50:52,337 --> 00:50:52,717
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not at all.

729
00:50:52,817 --> 00:50:56,300
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just hoping that watching the nostrils will result in fulfilling your wishes.

730
00:50:57,460 --> 00:50:59,662
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, you meditate with sensuality, as the Buddha said.

731
00:51:02,383 --> 00:51:03,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, by the way, you can't.

732
00:51:03,684 --> 00:51:06,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, not being aware of the context doesn't mean the context is not there.

733
00:51:06,946 --> 00:51:09,007
[SPEAKER_02]: It just means you're unaware of it.

734
00:51:09,027 --> 00:51:10,528
[SPEAKER_02]: It means you're ignoring it.

735
00:51:10,709 --> 00:51:12,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence avijjā, the ignorance, the atmas thing.

736
00:51:14,004 --> 00:51:14,464
[SPEAKER_02]: You can do.

737
00:51:14,725 --> 00:51:15,966
[SPEAKER_02]: It's indifferent to you.

738
00:51:16,326 --> 00:51:17,247
[SPEAKER_02]: It's indifferent to you, yeah.

739
00:51:17,808 --> 00:51:24,173
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the context of you needing a meditation method, a direct set of precise instruction, what to do, what to observe?

740
00:51:24,213 --> 00:51:25,795
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the context of that experience?

741
00:51:26,576 --> 00:51:27,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Desire.

742
00:51:27,877 --> 00:51:28,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Desire?

743
00:51:28,737 --> 00:51:29,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Rooted in what?

744
00:51:29,478 --> 00:51:30,299
[SPEAKER_02]: In how you feel?

745
00:51:30,379 --> 00:51:32,241
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you understanding these things?

746
00:51:32,721 --> 00:51:33,402
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you can't.

747
00:51:33,522 --> 00:51:34,503
[SPEAKER_02]: It's mutually exclusive.

748
00:51:34,983 --> 00:51:36,545
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll either start looking at the context,

749
00:51:37,328 --> 00:51:40,989
[SPEAKER_02]: And by doing so, become familiar with it, allow it to endure, recollect it.

750
00:51:41,569 --> 00:51:51,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollect your desire to be free from suffering, not when you go and sit to watch your nostrils, but when you want to go out with your friends and drink, or break precepts, or whatever else.

751
00:51:52,112 --> 00:51:59,774
[SPEAKER_02]: Or as a monk, you want to distract yourself with work, or society, or group, or other monks, socializing, or whatever else.

752
00:52:00,154 --> 00:52:02,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollect that context, and you won't do these things.

753
00:52:04,155 --> 00:52:05,356
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what mindfulness is.

754
00:52:05,456 --> 00:52:06,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like sitting and

755
00:52:08,122 --> 00:52:11,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Breathing in and out.

756
00:52:11,563 --> 00:52:18,624
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why mindfulness of breathing results in mindfulness being developed on the four, four, four reference points.

757
00:52:19,784 --> 00:52:20,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Satipaṭṭhāna.

758
00:52:21,805 --> 00:52:22,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing else.

759
00:52:22,325 --> 00:52:37,108
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, this anapasati fully developed only results in developing of the four foundations of mindfulness, which results in the four right strivings, which then results in development of the seven enlightenment factors, which basically makes you free from all passion and become an arahant.

760
00:52:42,223 --> 00:52:48,025
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you still have a need to focus on the tip of your nose when you're breathing, look at the context.

761
00:52:48,105 --> 00:52:48,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

762
00:52:49,606 --> 00:52:51,266
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where your mindfulness is.

763
00:52:51,286 --> 00:52:52,587
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not you looking in the breath.

764
00:52:53,127 --> 00:52:55,468
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you having a certain context making you do that.

765
00:53:01,550 --> 00:53:03,591
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can't just do pure mindfulness either.

766
00:53:04,431 --> 00:53:05,712
[SPEAKER_00]: The context is effortless.

767
00:53:08,504 --> 00:53:12,346
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, discerning the context, that's always effortless, because that's always there on its own.

768
00:53:13,126 --> 00:53:15,588
[SPEAKER_02]: What you do on account of the context, that's not effortless.

769
00:53:16,388 --> 00:53:18,449
[SPEAKER_02]: That's something you can be burdened by or freed from.

770
00:53:19,690 --> 00:53:23,552
[SPEAKER_02]: I must do this when, like, when it's this, or I don't have to do anything.

771
00:53:48,829 --> 00:53:52,852
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you know, the Buddha said many times, you can't have... you can't overdo mindfulness.

772
00:53:54,253 --> 00:53:57,995
[SPEAKER_02]: But if mindfulness is focusing practice... You can never do that.

773
00:53:58,355 --> 00:53:59,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you really not overdo that?

774
00:54:00,497 --> 00:54:00,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

775
00:54:01,677 --> 00:54:04,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you can very much overdo it, which means it's completely not that.

776
00:54:08,142 --> 00:54:12,564
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, why are you... why are you focusing?

777
00:54:12,584 --> 00:54:16,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you focusing on the touching points... And if you got it wrong... ...of your body on the chair?

778
00:54:16,187 --> 00:54:17,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you focusing on the nostrils?

779
00:54:19,605 --> 00:54:20,706
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would I be doing that?

780
00:54:22,446 --> 00:54:25,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Finding out why is discerning the context that's making you do it.

781
00:54:26,648 --> 00:54:28,089
[SPEAKER_02]: It's discerning the mindfulness.

782
00:54:28,269 --> 00:54:31,690
[SPEAKER_02]: It's recollecting that because of which you are acting in this manner.

783
00:54:31,710 --> 00:54:36,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Becoming aware of your responsibility, your action and everything else.

784
00:54:36,772 --> 00:54:44,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Not relying on the magical kind of results that will come out of this like mindless performance of these methods and techniques.

785
00:54:47,166 --> 00:54:47,587
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

786
00:54:47,727 --> 00:54:51,651
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do the method without the context that makes you do it.

787
00:54:51,751 --> 00:54:53,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And that context is where your view is.

788
00:55:07,550 --> 00:55:13,472
[SPEAKER_02]: And in this case, in that case, it would be the view of expecting results as at the end of this performance, of this set of motions.

789
00:55:13,492 --> 00:55:16,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Building up, building up the mindfulness, building up the mindfulness, building up.

790
00:55:16,293 --> 00:55:17,733
[SPEAKER_02]: And that would be a magic combination.

791
00:55:17,853 --> 00:55:23,595
[SPEAKER_02]: If you, if you understand the context, you can't lose it.

792
00:55:24,898 --> 00:55:25,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, not just that.

793
00:55:25,899 --> 00:55:41,807
[SPEAKER_02]: It's basically the fact that you you're implying that you can build up this focusing practice that would result in this energy release, means your view is of a nature that that puts the value of things that happen to you as a result of this what you do, which means your practice, then,

794
00:55:42,728 --> 00:55:45,491
[SPEAKER_02]: Results of your practice, they can come or not.

795
00:55:45,731 --> 00:55:46,552
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know for sure.

796
00:55:47,073 --> 00:55:47,853
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know how much.

797
00:55:48,094 --> 00:55:52,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Means there is no direct knowledge between this is what's done and this is the result of this passion between it.

798
00:55:53,159 --> 00:55:53,880
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't see that.

799
00:55:54,300 --> 00:55:58,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means there is no knowledge between suffering and what do I do to be free from it.

800
00:56:00,120 --> 00:56:06,044
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just I'm subject to suffering and I'm doing these things to one extent, how, when, I don't know.

801
00:56:06,685 --> 00:56:09,727
[SPEAKER_02]: I just have faith that doing this will free me from this.

802
00:56:10,548 --> 00:56:18,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, the beginning of the right doing of the sotāpanna is knowing exactly what you're supposed to do, how, how much and what it will result in.

803
00:56:19,394 --> 00:56:21,176
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, he knows the way out of suffering.

804
00:56:21,216 --> 00:56:29,122
[SPEAKER_02]: He's not just hoping for it through like performing sets of duties and virtues and observances and techniques and methods.

805
00:56:30,782 --> 00:56:31,542
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the difference.

806
00:56:31,962 --> 00:56:37,804
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is the context of... if you... if you're practicing your meditation with these expectations, that is your context.

807
00:56:38,304 --> 00:56:43,906
[SPEAKER_02]: You expect it to happen to you, which means you are... you don't know how you are supposed to develop it.

808
00:56:45,087 --> 00:56:47,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Those... that whole knowledge doesn't have to be...

809
00:56:50,748 --> 00:56:56,993
[SPEAKER_00]: To have that knowledge again, you don't have to build up time through focusing meditation.

810
00:56:58,094 --> 00:57:06,381
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you do one hour of meditation, two hours of meditation, you build up that knowledge again that you had when you did that two-week retreat, when you were ten hours a day.

811
00:57:07,402 --> 00:57:10,344
[SPEAKER_00]: But that, if you have the knowledge, it's always there.

812
00:57:10,364 --> 00:57:11,785
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't not lose it.

813
00:57:12,326 --> 00:57:13,307
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, eh...

814
00:57:14,479 --> 00:57:15,822
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to figure it out all again.

815
00:57:17,124 --> 00:57:20,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's the definition of the knowledge, yeah.

816
00:57:20,571 --> 00:57:26,261
[SPEAKER_02]: And you wouldn't have developed that knowledge by doing your technique for three hours.

817
00:57:26,301 --> 00:57:27,223
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as I said, again,

818
00:57:27,802 --> 00:57:34,828
[SPEAKER_02]: The point of it is taking you away from the context, putting the emphasis of it will happen to me, whatever I'm hoping for.

819
00:57:34,868 --> 00:57:36,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like I will develop it.

820
00:57:37,090 --> 00:57:42,334
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think you have to build up your understanding again, it means you don't understand anything.

821
00:57:42,574 --> 00:57:44,776
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have to figure it out, it means you don't.

822
00:57:44,916 --> 00:57:45,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Again.

823
00:57:45,497 --> 00:57:46,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you figured it out before.

824
00:57:46,898 --> 00:57:47,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

825
00:57:48,499 --> 00:57:52,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if you have to figure it out, genuinely figure it out, that's fine.

826
00:57:52,743 --> 00:57:56,546
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just how you're basically making effort to contemplate and overcome the doubt.

827
00:57:58,993 --> 00:58:06,175
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as long as we're not confusing the actual trying to understand for watching your nostrils, that's not even remotely close to that.

828
00:58:07,756 --> 00:58:16,859
[SPEAKER_02]: But yes, somebody who doesn't have fully confirmed confidence in his own knowledge, knowing right is right, wrong is wrong, will have to keep going back and doing it, and doing it, and doing it.

829
00:58:17,139 --> 00:58:19,600
[SPEAKER_02]: And when I say doing it, I mean trying to re-clarify it.

830
00:58:20,480 --> 00:58:21,301
[SPEAKER_02]: To understand this.

831
00:58:21,341 --> 00:58:22,221
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I regard this?

832
00:58:22,661 --> 00:58:23,561
[SPEAKER_02]: Passion has arisen.

833
00:58:23,621 --> 00:58:24,122
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that?

834
00:58:24,162 --> 00:58:25,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Why did I attend?

835
00:58:27,144 --> 00:58:29,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, by all means, do all that as much as you want.

836
00:58:30,186 --> 00:58:34,608
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it can result in restlessness, but it certainly did result in developing discernment.

837
00:58:35,609 --> 00:58:37,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you understood that mindfulness is effortless.

838
00:58:38,691 --> 00:58:40,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, well, that's a completely one thing.

839
00:58:41,212 --> 00:58:50,337
[SPEAKER_02]: But what I'm talking about is when people would like not even make the effort to understand, but expect understanding to come out of a mechanical observation of my nostrils.

840
00:58:51,378 --> 00:58:52,999
[SPEAKER_02]: Literally, belief is on that level.

841
00:58:55,802 --> 00:59:03,466
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's absolutely absolutely no, not even a trace of conflating that with trying to understand and actually discerning that which you're becoming familiar with.

842
00:59:04,306 --> 00:59:11,669
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're not becoming familiar with anything except with the greatness of the expectation that understanding will happen to me as a result of this.

843
00:59:12,810 --> 00:59:15,451
[SPEAKER_02]: But understanding doesn't happen to it, you develop it.

844
00:59:16,011 --> 00:59:19,673
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why Buddha said it cannot be taken away once it's developed, because he wasn't given.

845
00:59:21,118 --> 00:59:22,718
[SPEAKER_01]: To you, externally, by any means.

846
00:59:23,218 --> 00:59:27,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And once it's developed, all you're doing is discerning something that's there on its own.

847
00:59:27,759 --> 00:59:29,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it can be developed.

848
00:59:30,320 --> 00:59:30,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

849
00:59:32,140 --> 00:59:33,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And why also it can't be taken away?

850
00:59:33,680 --> 00:59:34,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's already there.

851
00:59:35,081 --> 00:59:35,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

852
00:59:35,661 --> 00:59:39,441
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a necessary basis for things that used to be taken away.

853
00:59:39,461 --> 00:59:41,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Even they were within that.

854
00:59:44,262 --> 00:59:48,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You are creating a context to your practice.

855
00:59:50,217 --> 00:59:51,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, exactly, yeah.

856
00:59:51,981 --> 00:59:56,773
[SPEAKER_02]: You're undoing the wrong context, which is, as I said, there always has to be some context.

857
00:59:56,913 --> 00:59:59,740
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's either wrong context or it's close to the right context.

858
01:00:00,622 --> 01:00:13,046
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by becoming aware of the context, even in spiritual context of responsibility, context of the lack of knowledge, context of being subjected to suffering all the time, even when I don't necessarily suffer, I'm still of the nature to suffer, I'm prey to suffering.

859
01:00:13,687 --> 01:00:17,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Let that context dwell in your mind and see how unwholesome or careless you get in the world.

860
01:00:18,068 --> 01:00:18,528
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't!

861
01:00:19,009 --> 01:00:21,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you will be on edge always, because you're always responsible.

862
01:00:24,130 --> 01:00:28,032
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, as the Buddha said, the noble search would be a natural result of it.

863
01:00:28,813 --> 01:00:32,216
[SPEAKER_02]: I need to do... like, this is the utmost... this is the only thing worth doing.

864
01:00:32,776 --> 01:00:36,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Doing something about this, what I'm subject to.

865
01:00:39,601 --> 01:00:40,902
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you get to learn more.

866
01:00:41,603 --> 01:00:50,430
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, then you're open to the... like with the... without looking for faults, you're open to the right instruction, and you will be able to hear it, when you come across it.

867
01:00:52,748 --> 01:00:59,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you have clarified, even as a puṭrjuna, the basis to which the right instruction applies.

868
01:01:01,074 --> 01:01:11,661
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't have that basis, you can have the Buddha face to face, telling you, you wouldn't see, you wouldn't know, and you wouldn't understand the value of what he said, as many cases in the suttas happened.

869
01:01:15,764 --> 01:01:21,608
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you have clarified the value of that context, of what you are subjected to, as a puṭrjuna,

870
01:01:22,405 --> 01:01:25,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Even a second-hand account of the Buddha's teaching would be enough for you.

871
01:01:30,310 --> 01:01:34,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you've done the work of uncluttering the mess, of ignoring.

872
01:01:35,953 --> 01:01:40,536
[SPEAKER_02]: And then further, you want to develop full understanding of that context is just more of the same.

873
01:01:41,417 --> 01:01:51,143
[SPEAKER_02]: More of uncluttering, more of protecting what you clarified, what you recollected, letting it endure until you become sufficiently familiar to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

874
01:01:59,102 --> 01:02:01,463
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how are you feeling right now?

875
01:02:01,483 --> 01:02:04,324
[SPEAKER_02]: It's basically recollect what's present enduring mood.

876
01:02:07,365 --> 01:02:12,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, can you recollect how long has that mood been enduring even before you recollected it?

877
01:02:13,167 --> 01:02:13,567
[SPEAKER_02]: You can.

878
01:02:14,048 --> 01:02:18,989
[SPEAKER_02]: Because recollection within recollection is again within the same recollection.

879
01:02:20,270 --> 01:02:28,133
[SPEAKER_02]: And if that thing were not enduring on its own, you wouldn't be able to remember it, how long it was there before.

880
01:02:31,670 --> 01:02:39,415
[SPEAKER_02]: So, recollect that you are still subject to suffering.

881
01:02:39,475 --> 01:02:46,280
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, when you sit, stand, walk, talk, eat, drink, sleep, whatever else, keep that context in your mind.

882
01:02:48,281 --> 01:02:50,863
[SPEAKER_02]: I am subject to suffering while I'm doing these things.

883
01:03:05,836 --> 01:03:08,298
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean, like, the sati is opposite of focusing.

884
01:03:10,059 --> 01:03:14,762
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't focus on the context for your intention to focus.

885
01:03:18,264 --> 01:03:25,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Trying to focus on the base of your focusing is like literally trying to see your eye with its own eye.

886
01:03:32,781 --> 01:03:36,103
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can see the harder that you're trying to focus, the less you see.

887
01:03:38,184 --> 01:03:38,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

888
01:03:39,304 --> 01:03:41,665
[SPEAKER_01]: And you might confuse that for some mystical experience.

889
01:03:42,185 --> 01:03:42,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

890
01:03:42,386 --> 01:03:45,607
[SPEAKER_02]: It goes hand in hand with the conflated context and so on.

891
01:03:47,628 --> 01:03:49,089
[SPEAKER_02]: But we spoke about it before.

892
01:03:49,269 --> 01:03:50,509
[SPEAKER_02]: You mentioned it as well.

893
01:03:51,410 --> 01:04:01,955
[SPEAKER_02]: When you come first and, you know, you live in the forest, the jungle, snakes and so on, until you're used to it, you're just like looking and everything, because you still don't have the context.

894
01:04:03,765 --> 01:04:08,029
[SPEAKER_02]: You have the context of the danger, but that needs to be clarified now.

895
01:04:08,530 --> 01:04:13,054
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not specific, so you don't really know what to look for, what is dangerous, what isn't.

896
01:04:14,736 --> 01:04:18,580
[SPEAKER_02]: So, okay, you know snakes are dangerous, but like even that, you think all the snakes are dangerous.

897
01:04:19,698 --> 01:04:21,059
[SPEAKER_02]: And there are actually very few that are.

898
01:04:21,879 --> 01:04:22,780
[SPEAKER_02]: And they are rarely seen.

899
01:04:22,840 --> 01:04:25,221
[SPEAKER_02]: But, initially, any snake means the same danger.

900
01:04:25,742 --> 01:04:29,904
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you end up going and, like, wary of everything, because that's your context.

901
01:04:29,944 --> 01:04:37,849
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, the more you dwell in that context... And, by the way, you don't need the snakes to focus on them for the context of the danger of the snake.

902
01:04:37,869 --> 01:04:38,609
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite the opposite.

903
01:04:38,729 --> 01:04:39,950
[SPEAKER_02]: The danger is when you don't see it.

904
01:04:39,990 --> 01:04:41,571
[SPEAKER_02]: When you see it, it's ten meters away.

905
01:04:41,591 --> 01:04:43,772
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not danger.

906
01:04:43,792 --> 01:04:48,615
[SPEAKER_02]: It's by not seeing it, by not being able to focus on it, that's where the context is discerned.

907
01:04:50,251 --> 01:04:52,514
[SPEAKER_02]: And once you become familiar with it, it becomes normal.

908
01:04:52,534 --> 01:04:59,083
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're not necessarily even feeling the the same weight or the same, like, mood in regard to that context, because it became the norm.

909
01:04:59,103 --> 01:05:00,545
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been doing it, you got used to it.

910
01:05:01,186 --> 01:05:02,247
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean it's not there.

911
01:05:03,069 --> 01:05:07,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as we were joking before, like, you stand like a blade of grass, sort of,

912
01:05:08,419 --> 01:05:13,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Touches your ankle with an unusual angle, and immediately your mind is on full.

913
01:05:14,120 --> 01:05:18,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you don't think, ah, I was touched, I must focus to my foot now, I must retract my foot.

914
01:05:19,382 --> 01:05:21,842
[SPEAKER_02]: As soon as it touches you, your foot is away from it.

915
01:05:23,043 --> 01:05:30,285
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you look and find out what it was, because the context of the danger is still there, and remains enduring there all this time.

916
01:05:30,545 --> 01:05:33,365
[SPEAKER_02]: The significance of the touch is already...

917
01:05:33,385 --> 01:05:36,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Within, seen against the backdrop of the right context.

918
01:05:36,975 --> 01:05:41,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Context that is important to you, that is significant to you, such as protect my life.

919
01:05:46,359 --> 01:05:52,903
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you do the mindfulness, if you do recollectiveness correctly, even your focusing will be effortless.

920
01:05:54,443 --> 01:05:55,464
[SPEAKER_02]: I focus on what is that.

921
01:05:57,465 --> 01:05:57,985
[SPEAKER_02]: It's effortless.

922
01:05:58,025 --> 01:05:58,925
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't require effort.

923
01:05:59,005 --> 01:06:02,147
[SPEAKER_02]: It will come naturally as a result of the rightly established context.

924
01:06:04,194 --> 01:06:06,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it trickles down.

925
01:06:06,535 --> 01:06:06,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

926
01:06:06,956 --> 01:06:10,778
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, this is what we're doing, guys, and then everyone starts doing their job.

927
01:06:10,798 --> 01:06:12,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to worry about it.

928
01:06:17,242 --> 01:06:21,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can't reverse-engineer it by hoping to focus, focus, focus somehow.

929
01:06:21,685 --> 01:06:25,487
[SPEAKER_02]: The context will be... Yeah, it will kind of miraculously emerge.

930
01:06:34,462 --> 01:06:37,743
[SPEAKER_02]: That would be true if there is no context at certain times.

931
01:06:38,963 --> 01:06:44,465
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you could be acting without context, then you could discern the context within your particular acts.

932
